/T (The program counts possible step sequences under the assumption that each move is exactly one or two steps and that every sequence is considered distinct by its order. It does not model blocked steps, variable step sizes or restrictions on consecutive moves. For extremely large n, the integer result itself becomes very large, so arithmetic and memory costs for the integer representation also increase.)
/E (The program counts possible step sequences under the assumption that each move is exactly one or two steps and that every sequence is considered distinct by its order. It does not model blocked steps, variable step sizes or restrictions on consecutive moves. For extremely large n, the integer result itself becomes very large, so arithmetic and memory costs for the integer representation also increase.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [28]
>>
